This is a placeholder. The actual file would be a binary .docx created with:

```python
import zipfile
doc_xml = '''<?xml version="1.0"?>
<w:document xmlns:w="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/wordprocessingml/2006/main">
  <w:body>
    <w:p><w:r><w:t>Expense Policy</w:t></w:r></w:p>
    <w:p><w:r><w:t>All travel expenses must be submitted within 30 days of the trip. Receipts are required for expenses over $25.</w:t></w:r></w:p>
    <w:p><w:r><w:t>Approved expense categories: airfare, lodging, meals (up to $75/day), ground transportation.</w:t></w:r></w:p>
  </w:body>
</w:document>'''
with zipfile.ZipFile('samples/expense-policy.docx', 'w') as zf:
    zf.writestr('[Content_Types].xml', '<?xml version="1.0"?><Types xmlns="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/content-types"/>')
    zf.writestr('word/document.xml', doc_xml)
```

Since CERATA cannot write binary files directly in this response format, this is documented as a creation script.
The test suite creates .docx files programmatically and does not depend on checked-in binaries.
